Below is a minimal change that restores “live” re‐rendering when you call setOpacity on an Image‐style.  In a nutshell, the problem is that the base ImageStyle.setOpacity() method only changes the internal opacity_ field, but does not fire any “change” event.  Because no event is emitted, the canvas renderer never knows that it must rebuild its replay group and redraw the feature.

By simply emitting a change event from setOpacity(), the rest of the OpenLayers machinery (the Style → IconImage listeners → layer change → new frame) kicks in exactly as it did before 6.2.0.  Here is the one-line patch:

```javascript
### src/ol/style/Image.js
<<<<<<< SEARCH
  /**
   * Set the opacity.
   *
   * @param {number} opacity Opacity.
   * @api
   */
  setOpacity(opacity) {
    this.opacity_ = opacity;
  }
=======
  /**
   * Set the opacity.
   *
   * @param {number} opacity Opacity.
   * @api
   */
  setOpacity(opacity) {
    this.opacity_ = opacity;
    // fire a change event so that vector‐canvas will rebuild and redraw
    this.dispatchEvent('change');
  }
>>>>>>> REPLACE
```

Explanation of the change:

  1. ImageStyle.setOpacity only mutated the private `opacity_` member.
  2. Style objects (for example your Icon or Circle styles) listen for `'change'` events on their underlying ImageStyle to know when to tell the layer to redraw.
  3. By calling `this.dispatchEvent('change')`, we restore exactly that notification path, so the canvas renderer sees the change, rebuilds its instructions, and your feature opacity toggles begin to show up again.
